WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.060
I've noticed that almost all the really good machine learning practitioners tend to have

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.400
a very sophisticated understanding of bias and variance.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.620
Bias and variance is one of those concepts that's easily learned but difficult to master.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.980
Even if you think you've seen the basic concepts of bias and variance, there's often more

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.260
nuance to it than you'd expect.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.020
In the deep learning era, another trend is that there's been less discussion of what's

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.100
called the bias-variance trade-off.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.900
You might have heard of this thing called the bias-variance trade-off.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.460
In the deep learning era, there's less of a trade-off.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.820
So we'd still talk about bias, we'd still talk about variance, but we just talk less

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.580
about the bias-variance trade-off.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.660
Let's see what this means.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.780
Let's see the dataset that looks like this.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.260
If you fit a straight line to the data, maybe you get a logistic regression fit to that.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.220
This is not a very good fit to the data, and so there's a class of high bias.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:56.820
So we say that this is underfitting the data.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:02.580
On the opposite end, if you fit an incredibly complex classifier, maybe a deep neural network

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:10.460
or a neural network with a, um, lot of hidden units, maybe you can fit the data perfectly,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.340
but that doesn't look like a great fit either.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.900
So this is a classifier with high variance and this is, um, overfitting the data.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.300
And there might be some classifier in between with a medium level of complexity that, you

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.260
know, maybe fits a curve like that, that looks like a much more reasonable fit to the data.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.900
And so that's the- we call that, you know, just right, right, somewhere in between.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:37.500
So in a 2D example like this with just two features, x1 and x2, you can plot the data

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.740
and visualize bias and variance.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.580
In high dimensional problems, um, you can't plot the data and visualize the decision

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.580
boundary.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.300
Instead, there are a couple of different metrics that we'll look at to try to understand

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.880
bias and variance.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.360
So considering our example of cat picture classification, where that's a positive example

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.360
and that's a negative example, the two key numbers to look at to understand bias and

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.680
variance will be the training set error and the depth set or the development set error.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.200
So for the sake of argument, let's say that, um, you know, recognizing cats and pictures

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.880
is something that people can do nearly perfectly, right?

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.280
And so let's say your training sets error is, um, 1%.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:25.440
And your depth set error is, uh, for the sake of argument, let's say it's 11%.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.480
So in this example, you're doing very well on the training set,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.240
but you're doing, um, relatively poorly on the development set.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.240
So this looks like you might have overfit the training set,

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.760
that somehow you're not generalizing well to

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.640
this holdout cross-validation set with the development set.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:50.560
And so if you have an example like this, we would say this has high variance.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.160
So by looking at the training set error and the development set error,

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.480
you know, you would be able to render a diagnosis of your algorithm having high variance.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.240
Now, let's say that you measured your training set and your depth set error,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.120
and, um, you get a different result.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.640
Let's say that your training set error is 15%.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.480
I'm writing your training set error in the top row,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.680
and your depth set error is 16%.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.520
In this case, assuming that humans, um, achieve, you know,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.400
roughly 0% error that humans can look at these pictures and just tell if it's cat or not,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.600
then it looks like the algorithm is not even doing very well on the training set.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.400
So if it's not even fitting the training data is seen that well,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.160
then this is underfitting the data,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.960
and so this algorithm has high bias.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.680
But in contrast, this is actually generalizing at a reasonable level to the depth set.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.400
Whereas performance on a depth set is only 1% worse than performance on the training set.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.960
So this algorithm has a problem of high bias because, um,

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.280
well, it's not even training, it's not even fitting the training set.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.000
Well, this is similar to the leftmost plot we had on the previous slide.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.360
Now, here's another example.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.160
Let's say that you have 15% training set error, so that's pretty high bias.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.120
But when you evaluate on a depth set,

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.480
it does even worse, maybe it does, you know, 30%.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:18.200
In this case, I would diagnose this algorithm as having high bias

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.800
because it's not doing that well on the training set and high variance.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.600
So this is, you know, really the worst of both worlds.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.360
Um, and one last example,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.680
if you have, you know,

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:35.200
0.5 training set error and 1% depth set error,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.760
then, well, maybe our users are quite happy that you have,

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.880
uh, CAD class 5 with only 1% error,

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.400
then this would have, you know, low bias and low variance.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.600
One subtlety that I'll just briefly mention,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.960
but we'll leave to a later video to discuss in detail,

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.640
is that this analysis is predicated on the assumption that

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.840
human level performance gets nearly 0% error.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.840
Or more generally, that the optimal error,

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.760
sometimes called Bayes' error for the,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:09.720
so the Bayesian optimal error is nearly 0%.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.560
I don't want to go into detail on this in this particular video,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.600
but it turns out that if the optimal error or the Bayes' error were much higher,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.040
say it were 15%, then if you look at this classifier,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.240
um, 15% is actually perfectly reasonable for a training set

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.040
and you wouldn't say that's high bias and we also have pretty low variance.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.920
So the case of how to analyze bias and variance when

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.600
no classifier can do very well.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.880
For example, if you have, um,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.720
really blurry images so that, you know,

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:45.920
even a human or just no system could possibly do very well,

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.640
then maybe Bayes' error is much higher and then there's

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.160
some details of how this analysis would change.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.720
But leaving aside this subtlety for now,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.320
the takeaway is that by looking at your training set error,

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.480
you can get a sense of how well you're fitting,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.200
at least the training data.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.760
And so that tells you if you have a bias problem.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.200
And then looking at how much higher your error goes,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.000
when you go from the training set to the depth set,

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.040
that should give you a sense of how bad is the variance problem.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.640
So you're doing a good job generalizing from the training set to the depth set.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.720
That gives you a sense of your variance.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.680
Um, all this is under the assumption that the Bayes' error is quite small

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.120
and that your training and your depth sets are drawn from the same distribution.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.200
If those assumptions are violated,

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.360
there's more sophisticated analysis you could do,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.240
which we'll talk about in the later video.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.760
Now, on the previous slide,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.160
you saw what high bias, high variance looks like.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.800
And I guess you had a sense of what a good classifier looks like.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.040
What does high bias and high variance looks like?

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.200
It's kind of the worst of both worlds.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.320
So you remember we said that a classifier like this,

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:57.720
a linear classifier has high bias because it underfits the data.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.680
So this would be a classifier that is mostly linear.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.040
And therefore underfits the data.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.600
We're drawing this in purple.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.680
But if somehow your classifier does some weird things,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.360
then it's actually overfitting parts of the data as well.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:19.840
So the classifier that I drew in purple has both high bias and high variance.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.360
Or it has high bias because by being a mostly linear classifier,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.520
it's just not fitting, you know, this quadratic light shape that well.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:31.200
But by having too much flexibility in the middle,

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.720
it somehow gets this example and this example overfits those two examples as well.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.560
So this classifier kind of has high bias because it was mostly linear,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.600
but you needed maybe a curve function, a quadratic function.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.640
And it has high variance because it had too much flexibility to fit,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.320
you know, those two mislabeled,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.880
although a lot of examples in the middle as well.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.840
In case this seems contrived,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.600
well, it is, this example is a little bit contrived in two dimensions,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.840
but with very high dimensional inputs,

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.720
you actually do get things with high bias in some regions and high variance in some regions.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:11.360
And so it is possible to get classifiers like this in high dimensional inputs that seem less contrived.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.720
So to summarize, you've seen how by looking at your algorithm's error on the training set

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.280
and your algorithm's error on the dev set,

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:24.000
you can try to diagnose whether it has problem of high bias or high variance or maybe both,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.440
or maybe neither.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.880
And depending on whether your algorithm suffers from bias or variance,

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.680
it turns out that there are different things you could try.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.560
So in the next video, I want to present to you a,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.120
what I call a basic recipe for machine learning that lets you more systematically

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.800
try to improve your algorithm,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.240
depending on whether it has high bias or high variance issues.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.080
So let's go on to the next video.
